\subsection*{Limitations}
We use a shorter estimation sample as we are constrained by macroeconomic data available for unified Germany.

\subsection*{Empirical Strategy}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Replicate Känzig's identification strategy by constructing the monthly oil supply news shock from high-frequency WTI futures price movements around OPEC announcements.
\item Replace WTI futures with Brent futures to construct an alternate measure.
\item Compare both series using correlation analysis and narrative evidence around major OPEC announcements.
\item Estimate a Proxy SVAR using the oil supply news shock as an external instrument for the structural oil supply shock.
\item Evaluate relevance of the instrument using first-stage statistics.
\item Estimate impulse response functions (IRFs) from the identified SVAR to analyse the effects on oil prices, world oil production, world oil inventories, world industrial production, German industrial production and German inflation.
\item Conduct robustness checks using Känzig's heteroskedasticity-based identification approach to assess whether the Brent-based surprise series isolates oil supply news shocks from unrelated sources of variation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Expected Contribution}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Identification Robustness: Examines whether the identification depends on the choice of oil benchmark (WTI versus Brent).
\item External Validity: By extending the study to Germany, our paper provides additional evidence on the effects of oil supply news shocks.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Expected Results}
We expect the Brent-based oil supply news shocks to be highly correlated with the original WTI-based shocks and identification to be robust to the choice of crude oil benchmark. For Germany, we expect positive oil supply news shocks (i.e., news of tighter future oil supply) to increase oil prices, reduce industrial production, and exert upward pressure on inflation.



